    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing the logarithms with a common base. We can use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). Using base 10, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten using the change of base formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(\\log 27\\). Since \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation now is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the fraction, we take the reciprocal of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\log x = 3 \\log 4\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\log a^b = b \\log a\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log x = \\log 4^3\n\\]\n\nSince the logarithms have the same base, we can equate the arguments:\n\n\\[\nx = 4^3\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
